    perf(buffer): batch box drawing, block elements, braille and dashes
    
    A run of printable text is handed to the buffer in one call, and
    `isSingleCellPrintable` decides what may be in the run. Box drawing and
    block elements were not in it, so every cell of a TUI's borders and bars
    took the per-code-point path - and there, since nothing short-circuits a
    symbol the way it does a letter, a full grapheme segmentation with three
    or four string allocations. Measured at 4.6 M characters/s against about
    155 for ASCII.
    
    The set now also takes U+2010..U+2027 (dashes, quotes, bullets),
    U+2500..U+259F (box drawing, block elements) and U+2800..U+28FF
    (braille). All are width 1 and Grapheme_Cluster_Break=Other, which is
    what a batched write needs; a new test checks both properties for every
    code unit the predicate accepts rather than for the ranges it is written
    in.
    
    Ten interleaved rounds, old against new, on an idle M1 Pro:
    
      box drawing    4.6 -> 125.5 M chars/s
      utf8 (Turkish)  82 -> 136 MiB/s   (the em dash in each line)
    
    ascii, ascii-long-lines, sgr, cyrillic and altscreen are unchanged, with
    overlapping ranges in every column of `bin/parse_bench.dart`.
    
