Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sat Aug 8 19:08:11 2026 +0300

    feat: draw caller-supplied prediction text at the cursor
    
    A Mosh client wants to show a keystroke before the server echoes it. Writing
    that character into the buffer would be wrong: mosh sends a screen diff
    computed against the frame it believes the client has, so a locally invented
    cell is never repaired and a wrong guess stays on screen for good.
    
    The widget already did the right thing for IME composition — text at the
    cursor, underlined, outside the buffer. This exposes the same painting to the
    caller as `TerminalView.predictionText`. The package learns nothing about
    prediction protocols; it is just text to show at the cursor.
    
    _paintComposingText is generalised into _paintCursorText rather than copied,
    so both callers share the colour resolution, reverse-video handling and
    horizontal placement instead of drifting apart later.
    
    Composition wins when both are present.
    
    Predictions are gated on the cursor's row being visible rather than on the
    cursor being painted, which is why _CursorPaintState gains isRowVisible.
    Hanging them off shouldPaint would make predicted text blink along with the
    cursor and disappear entirely whenever an application hides it — for
    composition that never showed, because composing text forces the cursor
    visible, and predictions must not do that: they are present continuously
    while typing and would suppress the blink altogether.
    
    debugCursorTextPaints counts inside the paint helper rather than recomputing
    the gate, so the test pinning that behaviour keeps failing if the gate in the
    paint pass changes. Verified it does: with the gate moved back to
    shouldPaint, the test fails.

