    perf(parser): batch writes for alphabets other than English
    
    The parser wrote a run of printable ASCII into cells in one call and handed
    everything else to `writeChar`, one code point at a time. The first accented
    letter ended the run, and every letter after it started a run of its own; an
    alphabet that is entirely non-ASCII never batched at all. `bin/parse_bench.dart`
    put Cyrillic at 6 MiB/s against ASCII's 92.
    
    Runs are now defined by `isSingleCellPrintable`, which admits ASCII, Latin-1,
    Latin Extended-A and B, IPA, spacing modifiers, Greek, Cyrillic and Armenian,
    minus the combining blocks inside that span and U+00AD SOFT HYPHEN. Every code
    point in it is a spacing character of width 1 and has
    Grapheme_Cluster_Break=Other, which are exactly the two properties a batched
    write needs: one code unit is one cell, and no unit in the run can continue the
    cluster before it.
    
    The same predicate replaces the U+0300 cut added in the previous commit, so a
    character outside a run costs no more than one inside it, and the two paths
    agree on what "cannot join" means.
    
    Cyrillic 6 -> 73 MiB/s, Turkish 32 -> 54. CJK stays on the per-code-point path
    by design: it is width 2 and needs the lead-plus-placeholder write.
    
