    most one character on the assumption it was a keyboard-trapped, already-
    resolved keypress. Genuine multi-step compositions (Gboard Turkish dead
    keys, pinyin) also open with exactly one character, so that first char was
    being emitted as a literal before the IME's correction/continuation ever
    arrived.
    
    Replace the length check with range-identity tracking: a composing range
    that keeps extending in place (same base, growing text, or a >1-char
    buffer being replaced by a shorter candidate) is treated as a live preview
    and held pending; a fresh single-character range that replaces a pending
    one without ever growing or collapsing is the f6568e1 shape and is flushed
    immediately, preserving the trapped-keystroke guarantee.
    
    One case remains unresolved and is documented with a skipped test: a
    composing range that opens with one character and is retroactively
    rewritten to a different single character before ever growing (e.g. a
    literal 'g' -> 'ğ' step with no third event) is byte-for-byte identical in
    shape to two independent f6568e1-trapped keystrokes, so it cannot be told
    apart without additional signal the platform doesn't provide here.
    
