Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Mon Aug 3 22:43:19 2026 +0300

    fix(ime): reconcile composition with backspace in deleteDetection mode
    
    deleteDetection previously split behaviour in two mutually exclusive
    ways: with it on, backspace worked but every composing update (Gboard
    Turkish dead-key rewrites, CJK pinyin candidates) was emitted as a
    literal insert immediately, so composition text reached the terminal
    broken and duplicated; with it off, composition was correct but a
    backspace at offset 0 (nothing left in the local buffer) produced
    onInsert('') instead of a delete signal.
    
    Both settings now defer emission while value.composing is a genuine,
    still-open multi-character preview and emit only the text the IME
    actually commits, once composing closes - deletion in deleteDetection
    mode is still derived from how much of the sentinel padding is left,
    not from a raw text-length diff.
    
    The one case that must NOT defer is the pattern f6568e1 fixed: some
    Android keyboards (Gboard, Samsung, SwiftKey) wrap an already-resolved
    single keypress in a composing range that never collapses on its own.
    That shape is distinguished from a real preview by opening fresh (no
    composition already tracked) with exactly one character, versus a real
    preview which opens with the word/syllable being built; it's committed
    immediately instead of waiting for a collapse that will never come, so
    trapped keystrokes still land exactly once with no double insertion.
    
    deleteDetection stays a flag rather than being fully unified: mode
    false has no sentinel and doesn't need the pending-composition state
    machine at all, just an added no-op-at-floor case to make its own
    backspace signal work.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

