Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Mon Aug 3 19:41:39 2026 +0300

    fix(terminal): batch hyperlink eviction scans instead of scanning per line
    
    8c985b7 resolved each evicted scrollback line's hyperlink ids against both
    buffers immediately, on every eviction. That's fine when the evicted id is
    still referenced elsewhere (an early exit short-circuits the scan), but the
    worst case is also the common case for hyperlink-heavy output - `ls
    --hyperlink=auto`, build logs, test runners - where every line carries its
    own distinct, never-reused hyperlink. There the early exit never fires and
    every single eviction pays a full O(buffer size) scan, turning scrolling
    into O(evictions * buffer size) work: with defaults (maxLines=1000, ~80
    columns, two buffers) that's on the order of 160,000 cell reads per evicted
    line.
    
    Fix: accumulate evicted hyperlink ids in _pendingEvictedHyperlinkIds (cheap,
    bounded by line length, still happens on every eviction) and only spend a
    buffer scan once _hyperlinkEvictionBatchSize (128) distinct ids have piled
    up, resolving all of them in that one pass. This amortizes one O(buffer
    size) scan across up to 128 evictions instead of paying it per eviction, so
    average cost per evicted hyperlink drops to roughly O(buffer size / batch
    size). 128 was chosen as a modest slice (~3%) of the 4096-entry ceiling:
    large enough to amortize well, small enough that at most 127 dead
    hyperlinks are ever resident between scans - a small fraction of the
    ceiling this mechanism exists to avoid hitting. The existing ceiling-
    triggered _pruneUnusedHyperlinks() bulk scan is authoritative over the
    whole registry when it runs, so it now also drains the pending batch as a
    free side effect.
    
    Also add an _explicitHyperlinkKeyByHyperlinkId reverse map so both the new
    batched resolver and the pre-existing _pruneUnusedHyperlinks() can remove
    stale _explicitHyperlinkIds entries in time proportional to what's actually
    being removed, instead of copying and scanning the whole map every time
    something is pruned.
    
    The scan loops now use indexed buffer.lines[i] access with an explicit
    break once nothing remains to resolve, since (as noted in review)
    buffer.lines.forEach's callback-based `return` only skips that line's body
    and does not stop the traversal.
    
    Adds a @visibleForTesting debugHyperlinkEvictionScanCells counter and a new
    test that writes thousands of lines each carrying a distinct hyperlink,
    then asserts the total cell-inspection count stays far below what an
    unbatched scan-per-eviction implementation would produce. Verified this
    test fails (returns ~4,032,060 cell inspections against a 200,000 bound)
    when the batch size is temporarily forced to 1 to reproduce 8c985b7's
    per-eviction behavior. Also loosened the prior eviction test's bound
    (50 -> 300) since batching means up to ~127 stale entries can now be
    resident at once by design, instead of being resolved immediately.
    
