Author: klc <m_kilic@outlook.com.tr>
Date:   Sun Aug 2 16:29:35 2026 +0300

    feat(rendering): render font ligatures behind an opt-in flag
    
    Terminal cells were painted one glyph at a time with every glyph-merging
    font feature explicitly disabled, so ligature fonts rendered no ligatures
    at all. Merging is what breaks a terminal grid, though, not shaping: a
    ligature that does not fill the cells it covers shifts every glyph after
    it on the row.
    
    Take a run-based detour instead of replacing the per-cell path. Cells are
    grouped into a run only when they paint identically today -- same
    attributes, colors and hyperlink, no cursor cell, no combining mark, no
    wide or framed cell -- and only when every codepoint is ASCII punctuation
    that programming ligatures are built from, so ordinary text never leaves
    the fast path. The run is shaped once and drawn only if its advance
    matches the cells it covers; otherwise it is discarded and the cells are
    painted individually. Both the cell size and the run advance come from
    the same font at the same size, which makes that check exact rather than
    approximate.
    
    Runs that fail the check are remembered instead of cached, so a font
    without ligatures cannot fill the paragraph cache with paragraphs that
    are never drawn. That memo is invalidated wherever the cache is, since a
    run rejected under one font may ligate under the next.
    
    Off by default, and inert unless the resolved font ships the features:
    none of the default fallback families do. Ligature rendering itself was
    verified locally against JetBrains Mono, where `=>`, `!=`, `===` and
    `-->` shape with a zero advance delta; the tests cover the guard, the run
    splitting rules and the grid invariant, since the test font has no
    ligatures to draw.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

